WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.292
A chart going round says no open model since late August uses

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:05.872
full attention in every layer.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:06.820
Is that true?

00:00:09.707 --> 00:00:10.584
Hi, I'm Vesna!

00:00:10.584 --> 00:00:13.799
I read the config file of every model on the chart's

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:14.968
list, and many more.

00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:15.845
Let's count layers.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.136
Chapter one.

00:00:18.136 --> 00:00:19.602
The chart and its source.

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:26.313
Here is the chart as posted.

00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:28.493
The drawings carry Sebastian Raschka's watermark.

00:00:28.913 --> 00:00:33.405
Most models were full attention throughout in twenty twenty-five, about a quarter

00:00:33.405 --> 00:00:36.400
early this year, and none since late August.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.646
The counts match Raschka's architecture gallery.

00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:43.604
I classified every layer of every gallery model from its config.

00:00:43.604 --> 00:00:45.520
The three bars come out exactly.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.394
The chart is an honest picture of the gallery.

00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:50.598
Chapter two.

00:00:50.598 --> 00:00:52.177
What counts as full attention?

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.778
Full attention: every new token scores every earlier key.

00:00:59.778 --> 00:01:02.328
Grouped query and latent attention still count.

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:05.607
They shrink each entry, but keep one per token.

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:08.663
A window layer reads only the last few tokens.

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:12.315
A sparse layer lets an indexer pick the top few, and

00:01:12.315 --> 00:01:13.490
attention reads only those.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.603
It can still reach any token, but the chart counts it as not full.

00:01:19.192 --> 00:01:20.861
Here is what a full layer costs.

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:22.825
Two, for a key and a value.

00:01:24.133 --> 00:01:25.926
Times the number of key value heads.

00:01:26.402 --> 00:01:27.817
Times the width of each head.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.601
Times the bytes per number.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:34.299
And every token of context adds that again, in every full layer.

00:01:36.283 --> 00:01:39.677
Here is the census as strips, one cell per layer.

00:01:39.677 --> 00:01:43.411
K2 Horizon, from this autumn: every layer is plain full attention.

00:01:43.831 --> 00:01:47.678
Gemma four: five window layers, then one full layer, over and over.

00:01:48.098 --> 00:01:51.703
Qwen three point eight: three linear layers that fold the past into

00:01:51.703 --> 00:01:53.806
a fixed state, then one full layer.

00:01:54.226 --> 00:01:58.182
Kimi K three, the chart's own example: the same pattern, with Kimi

00:01:58.182 --> 00:02:00.159
Delta Attention as the linear layer.

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:04.552
K three point one Pro keeps every tensor, but its twenty-four full

00:02:04.552 --> 00:02:05.877
layers gained an indexer.

00:02:05.877 --> 00:02:07.201
They are still there.

00:02:07.201 --> 00:02:08.857
They stopped counting as full.

00:02:09.277 --> 00:02:11.665
MiniMax M three had three full layers.

00:02:11.665 --> 00:02:14.734
M three point one Flash made them sparse too.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.099
Same backbone, new class.

00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:18.785
Chapter three.

00:02:18.785 --> 00:02:19.763
A wider list.

00:02:25.108 --> 00:02:27.466
The gallery draws architectures worth drawing.

00:02:27.466 --> 00:02:32.182
So I added sixty-five open releases from this site's coverage, and dropped

00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:34.541
fine-tunes that reuse another model's layers.

00:02:34.961 --> 00:02:36.852
The first two windows barely move.

00:02:36.852 --> 00:02:40.003
The last one does: fourteen point three percent, not zero.

00:02:40.423 --> 00:02:41.571
The decline is real.

00:02:41.571 --> 00:02:43.294
The zero belongs to the list.

00:02:44.883 --> 00:02:49.500
Five of thirty-five: three K2 Horizon models, Nanbeige and MiniCPM.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:50.424
Mostly small.

00:02:50.424 --> 00:02:53.655
By parameters, full attention is nearly gone.

00:02:55.142 --> 00:02:57.551
The second half of the claim needs a closer look.

00:02:57.551 --> 00:02:58.997
What is in that last bar?

00:03:00.758 --> 00:03:01.417
Chapter four.

00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:04.053
No full attention is not no global attention.

00:03:07.967 --> 00:03:11.914
Every no full attention model since August keeps a layer like this.

00:03:11.914 --> 00:03:15.533
The query meets a small index key for every earlier token.

00:03:15.953 --> 00:03:19.833
The indexer keeps the best few hundred to two thousand tokens or blocks.

00:03:20.253 --> 00:03:21.520
Attention reads only those.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.637
But the whole cache is kept, because any token might be picked next.

00:03:26.057 --> 00:03:30.396
So sparse layers cut what each step reads, not what the server stores.

00:03:30.396 --> 00:03:33.733
All eleven such models can still reach every earlier token.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:40.011
Kimi K three's own diagram: three delta layers, then one latent attention layer.

00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:44.473
Add an indexer to those, and the chart calls it no full attention.

00:03:46.342 --> 00:03:46.885
Chapter five.

00:03:46.885 --> 00:03:48.786
What it buys, and what it costs.

00:03:53.217 --> 00:03:57.068
One full layer of Qwen three point eight keeps four thousand and

00:03:57.068 --> 00:03:58.351
ninety-six bytes per token.

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.957
Sixteen of them: sixty-four kibibytes.

00:04:01.877 --> 00:04:05.739
At its full context, seventeen point two gigabytes for one sequence.

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:08.309
All full, it would be sixty-eight point seven.

00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:12.477
The other forty-eight layers hold a fixed state of a hundred and

00:04:12.477 --> 00:04:13.954
forty-four mebibytes, at any length.

00:04:15.508 --> 00:04:17.067
Sparse is the opposite trade.

00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:20.810
At a million tokens MiniMax keeps more than plain attention would, but

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.681
each step reads about an eighth.

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:25.676
What does reading less cost?

00:04:25.676 --> 00:04:29.381
With the true top keys, Qwen keeps ninety-eight percent of its long-context

00:04:29.381 --> 00:04:31.850
score down to five hundred and twelve keys.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.383
Single needles survive.

00:04:33.383 --> 00:04:36.352
Tasks that need many scattered tokens fail first.

00:04:37.767 --> 00:04:39.841
Open models are not dropping global attention.

00:04:39.841 --> 00:04:43.099
They are putting an indexer, a window or a fixed state

00:04:43.099 --> 00:04:44.284
in front of it.

00:04:46.817 --> 00:04:47.585
To recap.

00:04:47.585 --> 00:04:49.890
The chart reproduces from the configs.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.195
Full attention survives in small models.

00:04:52.195 --> 00:04:55.268
And no full attention usually means an indexer.

00:04:55.688 --> 00:04:57.788
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:57.788 --> 00:04:58.388
I'm Vesna.

00:04:58.388 --> 00:04:58.688
Bye!
